|> Exercise
    handle=_lines_of_slope_1_over_2_

    True or false: Lines of slope $-{1 \over 2}$
    are perpendicular to lines of slope $2$.

    |> Solution

        This is true, as illustrated by the following
        pair of lines:

        |> Image
            src=images/c2_slope_two_and_minus_one_half.svg
            class=relative w-fit

        |> ImageRight
            src=images/c2_slope_two_and_minus_one_half_cloud_right.svg
            offset_y=20%
            offset_x=-5%

        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/c2_slope_two_and_minus_one_half_cloud_left.svg
            offset_x=-25%
            offset_y=47%

        In more detail, the two triangles are related
        by a $90^\circ$ rotation and so, likewise,
        are the lines defined by their hypotenuses!

        |> SolutionNote

            More generally, a line of slope $p$ is
            perpendicular to a line of slope $-1/p$, for
            all $p \ne 0$. By a similar drawing:

            |> Image
                src=images/c2_slope_p_minus_one_over_p.svg
                class=relative w-fit

                !! |> ImageRight
                !!     src=images/c2_slope_p_minus_one_over_p_cloud_right.svg
                !!     offset_y=28%
                !!     offset_x=-5%

                !! |> ImageLeft
                !!     src=images/c2_slope_p_minus_one_over_p_cloud_left.svg
                !!     offset_x=-18%
                !!     offset_y=44%

            |> StarDivider

        !! (If the axes are oriented the usual way then
        !! the above drawing covers all the cases $p > 0$,
        !! whereas if we flip the two number axes to point
        !! down/left the above drawing covers all the cases
        !! $p < 0$—magic!)
